Thanks very much  to everyone who replied --I had several excellent answers
(summarized below) all of which seem to get the job done, with some variation
in the input options (some allow you to specify the width of the ci
explicitly), i tried three which all worked equally well and produced the same
answers.

-original question-
Hello, 

Does anyone know if is there function in S+ to calculate exact confidence
intervals for proportions based on binomial probability distribution (e.g. k
successes in n trials). Are the confidence intervals reported by prop.test
exact? 

Thanks much in advance.

Tim

____________________________________________________________________________
__________

Try this: 
binom.conf<-function(r,n){ 
#95% exact binom conf int from Armitage and Berry '94 (pg 121) 
#if (r==0) lower<-0 
lower<-ifelse(r==0,0,r/(r+(n-r+1)*qf(.975,(2*(n-r+1)),(2*r)))) 
#if (r==n) upper<-1 
upper<-ifelse(r==n,1,(r+1)/(r+1+(n-r)/qf(.975,(2*(r+1)),(2*(n-r))))) 
return(list(lower=lower,upper=upper))}

_____________________________________

exact.binom.CI <- 
function(P, N, k) 
{ 
# Exact Binomial Condfidence limits. ; 
# To get 95% limits, enter (0.95, trials, successes); 
# Ref: Clopper & Pearson, Biometrika (1934), v26, p.404 ; 
if(N > k & k >= 1) return(c(pl = qbeta((1 - P)/2, k, N - k + 1), pu 
= qbeta(1 - (1 - P)/ 
2, k + 1, N - k))) 
if(N > 0 & N == k) 
return(c(pl = P^(1/n), pu = 1)) 
if(N > 0 & k == 0) 
return(c(pl = 0, pu = (1 - P^(1/N))))

______________________________________

binomCI 
function(n, k, alpha1 = 0.05, alpha2 = 0.05) 
{ 
#This function finds Confidence Intervals (L, U) on a binomi 
al 
#parameter p, so that P(L <= p) >= 1 - alpha1, 
# and P(p <= U) >= 1 - alpha2 
nu1 <- 2 * (n + 1 - k) 
nu2 <- 2 * k 
if(k == 0) 
p1 <- 0 
else p1 <- 1/(1 + (nu1/nu2) * qf(1 - alpha1, nu1, 
nu2)) 
nu1 <- 2 * (k + 1) 
nu2 <- 2 * (n - k) 
if(k == n) 
p2 <- 1 
else p2 <- (W <- (nu1/nu2) * qf(1 - alpha2, nu1, 
nu2))/(1 + W) 
c(p1, p2) 
}

______________________________

The Hmisc library has the binconf function which computes the 
exact CL and the less conservative Wilson interval. 
See http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/Hmisc.html 
- the entry under Online Help Documentation.

____________________________

Collett, D. Modeling Binary Data, London: Chapman & Hall (1991), pp 23-25.
It is also referenced as the source for a SAS macro EXACTPCI, V1.2
> binom.conf 
function(y, n, alpha = 0.05) 
{ 
if(y == 0) 
lower <- 0 
else lower <- y/(y + (n - y + 1) * qf(1 - (alpha/2), 2 * (n - y + 1), 2 * y)) 
if(y == n) 
upper <- 1 
else upper <- (y + 1)/(y + 1 + (n - y)/qf(1 - (alpha/2), 2 * (y + 1), 2 * (n -
y))) 
cbind(lower, upper) 
}

