.BG 
.FN cibinom 
.TL 
Confidence limits for the binomial 
.DN 
Give exact confidence limits for a binomial parameter 
.CS 
cibinom(k, n, p=0.95, method=c("exact", "arcsin", "normal", "anscombe")) 
.RA 
.AG k 
number of successes 
.AG n 
number of trials 
.OA 
.AG p 
probability level for the (two-sided) interval 
.AG method 
the method of approximation. The approximate methods are 
supplied for comparison purposes. 
.RT 
Either a vector containing the lower and upper limits, or a two column 
matrix of lower and upper limits. 
.DT 
The exact method is based on equation 10.7 of Feller, which relates 
binomial probabilities to tail area of the beta distribution. 
The arcsin method is based on the approximation that asin(sqrt(p)) 
is approximately normal with a variance of .25/n. 
The Anscombe approximation is a refinement of the arcsin method. 
The normal or Gaussian approximation assumes that p is normal with 
variance p(1-p)/n. It is known to be poor. 
The arcsin, Anscombe, and normal approximations incorporate a continuity 
correction. 
.SH REFERENCES 
W.F. Feller (1950). An Introduction to Probability Theory and its 
Applications, Volume 1, Chapter 6, Wiley. 
.br 
F.J. Anscombe (1949). Transformations of Poisson, binomial and 
negative-binomial data. Biometrika, vol 35, p246-254. 
.SA 
pbinom, qbinom 
.EX 
> cibinom(4,39) # 95% confidence limit 
lower upper 
0.02866011 0.2422097 
> pbinom(4,39,0.2422097) #chance of seeing 4 or fewer events with large p 
[1] 0.02500002 
> 1-pbinom(3,39,0.02866011) #chance of seeing 4 or more, with small p 
[1] 0.02500001 
.KW distribution 
.WR 
