From Bill.Venables at cmis.csiro.au  Sun May  7 04:16:59 2000
From: Bill.Venables at cmis.csiro.au (Bill Venables)
Date: Sun, 07 May 2000 14:16:59 +1000
Subject: Re: [S] Permutation of n Numbers
Message-ID: <3.0.32.20000507141638.0126b2d0@pophost.nsw.cmis.csiro.au>

Your problem is that you clobber one of the values in p as you are
swapping.  You should probably be using replace() just once with 

p <- replace(p, c(k, j), p[c(j, k)])

In fact, lets check:

> Permu
function(n)
{
        k <- n
        p <- 1:n
        repeat {
                u <- runif(1)
                j <- floor(k * u) + 1
                p <- replace(p, c(k, j), p[c(j, k)])
                k <- k - 1
                if(k < 1)
                        return(p)
        }
}
>
> Permu(5)
[1] 1 3 2 4 5
> Permu(5)
[1] 3 1 5 2 4
> Permu(5)
[1] 4 3 2 1 5

But the function is unnecessary since there is already a very quick one to
do the same job built-in:

> sample(5)
[1] 3 2 1 4 5

