Greetings S-Plus Users:

While a long-time user of S-Plus, I am new to Spatial Stats, and have an
operational question.

If I understand things correctly (and I may not), the Spatial Stats module
will allow me to describe a random field based on measured observations.  It
will further let me simulate the response at a point WITHIN THAT SPECIFIC
RANDOM FIELD.  Repeated simulations of that point will be identical.

Perhaps I am fortunate in having copious data from many, nominally
identical, random fields (surface topological measurements of different
parts manufactured  to a common print).  I wish to simulate an entire random
field, representing a new part, having the same spatial relationships
exhibited by its sister parts, no two of which, of course, being identical,
although S-Plus descriptions of their random fields are similar.

How can I simulate an entire random field, one that isn't simply a
recapitulation of the input covariance structure?

Thank you.

