Jeffrey J. Hallman [SMTP:m1jjh00 at frb.gov] wrote:

> Underscores (in C) and dots (in S) in variable names are also an
> abomination.
> These are both case-sensitive languages, so you should take advantage of
> that
> to write
> 
> aVeryLongVariableName
> 
> rather than
> 
> a_very_long_variable_name  (in C)
> 
> or
> 
> a.very.long.variable.name (in S).
> 
> Jeff

Ah, but eschewing underscores (and dots) actually limits your
ability to exploit case sensitivity.  I frequently use case as a
visual clue to an object's scope:

   Nice                         Yuck
   -----------------            ---------------
   a.local.var                  alocalvar
   Class.Data                   ClassData
   GLOBAL.DATA.TABLE            GLOBALDATATABLE

Exploiting case without _/. is even harder when there are
acronyms you'd like to use:

   Nice                         Yuck
   -----------------            ---------------
   DOE.data.EREN.table          DOEDataERENTable

I'm still grieving that S 4 (Splus 5) leans so heavily on
mixed-case-only names.

--Todd
